{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791370081,"nonce":"EQsAinRgHaXBpFbSJVxVnuuvH88=","title":"Abstract wrappers of Iterator\u003cT\u003e lose the iteration handle","message":"At cbc9eaa25063af9179c374c9085ca333c23e3ba1, Issue4809 crashes in its for-in loop over abstract Iter\u003cT\u003e(Iterator\u003cT\u003e) from/to Iterator\u003cT\u003e. MIR iter_protocol_of follows aliases and generic instances but stops at Abstract, so a value entering the abstract is not treated as the underlying protocol handle and the loop falls through to array lowering. The abstract is transparent here; it declares no iterator/hasNext/next override.\n\nRecognize a transparent abstract's underlying Iterator/Iterable protocol at the same argument/local boundaries and loop dispatch as that underlying type. An abstract declaring its own iteration methods must keep those methods rather than inheriting a handle representation.\n\nRecover the bound underlying element type for Int/Float/String loops and comprehensions, including typedef wrappers, with alias and abstract generic substitution. The first probe fixed Issue4809 and Int/Float paths but exposed a String typedef loop still typed Dynamic; following its target with alias bindings fixes that case too.\n\nAbstractIteratorProtocol.hx tests zero Int, Int arguments, Float summation, String/empty String through a typedef, and array comprehensions. Native Haxe --interp and pinned Cranelift v2 pass. Logs: /tmp/rayzor-iteration-pinned-v2.log and /tmp/rayzor-iteration-focused-v1/report.tsv.\n\nParent iterator issues d925c9c1eb61674d597ae81bad2d3b9220cc3911f0bbadd9d7c3e3285e2c30b4 and 09019f0e4bbfda7cbd56240093019d543abc03ef03c18c622e729773ed378c04 also cover mutating abstract next()/this writeback (Issue3539), which remains outside this fix. Native Haxe rejects for-in over a non-forwarding abstract Iterable\u003cT\u003e; the fixture therefore covers the accepted transparent Iterator\u003cT\u003e case rather than assuming the same source semantics for both protocols.","files":null}]}